{
  "comment": "The driver's framing (C10): the frames the browser driver (apps/locus/keeper/cmd/cyfr-browser, Go) and its one host (Opus.Browser.Frame, Elixir) carry over the driver's terminal session, each way. A frame is 0x1E, \"B1\", the body's length in bytes as 8 lowercase hex digits, the first 8 bytes of the body's SHA-256 as 16 lowercase hex digits, the body, and 0x0A. The body is one JSON object, UTF-8, holding no byte below 0x20 (JSON escapes them), so 0x1E never appears inside a body and a reader that loses its place takes up again at the next one. `encode` gives a body and the exact frame every side writes for it. `decode` gives the pieces of a stream as they arrive, in order, with the reader's bound on a body's length (`max`): the bodies it takes, in order, and the bytes it still holds waiting at the end (`rest`). A reader drops every byte before a 0x1E; it skips a 0x1E whose header does not read (a byte that is not a lowercase hex digit, or a length of 0 or past `max`), whose body's digest differs, or whose body is not followed by 0x0A, or whose claimed body holds a 0x1E (the frame was cut), and takes up at the next 0x1E after it; and it holds an incomplete frame until more bytes come. `messages` names each message, with the fields it carries. `bye` is the driver's last message, once the Chrome it started has exited, so what Chrome writes as it closes, its profile and a network log, is written; a host may pass it over. A cast (C12) is one window's screencast: a `req` of op `cast` asks for it, with the width and height its images are scaled to fit, and its id names the cast from then on. Each `image` is one JPEG in base64, in one message, never chunked, so an image is bounded by `max_to_host`; the driver sends the next only once the host has said `seen` of the last, and an image lost in a tail is never sent again. `uncast` ends a cast, from the host, or from the driver when its window closed. `input` carries the person's events to the cast's window, at most `input.max_events` in one message and `input_in_flight` messages of one cast on their way at once at every hop (the page hands the next only once the host took the last, the host writes the next only once the last is written, and the driver holds no more than that many waiting behind the one it dispatches; what comes beyond is dropped, never queued), each an event `input.accept` shows and none `input.refuse` shows: a mouse button or move, a wheel or a key, with exactly its kind's fields, its place a fraction of the image from its top left. A response's text, its chunks put together, is at most `max_response` bytes: a driver sends none longer, and a host keeps no more of one than that as it assembles it, whatever the driver sends. A put (C15) is a `req` of op `page` whose args are the page's `root` (`/t/<athanor>/local/<name>`), its `entry` and its `files`, each a `path` relative to the root (no leading slash), its `type` and its `size` in bytes, and whose `of` says how many `part` messages follow it: each carries, in base64, from `seq` 0 up in order, the next at most `put.part_bytes` bytes of the files' bytes laid end to end in the manifest's order. The driver runs the put once its last part has arrived and the sizes add up, and answers it as a page, with the window's address and title; parts out of order, sizes that do not add up, more than `put.max_files` files or more than `put.max_bytes` bytes answer an error (`bad_request`, or `too_large` past the bound) and keep nothing. A put is the only page the driver shows. From then until the window shows another page or closes, a GET or HEAD for `https://cyfr.invalid` from that window, its frames and its workers is answered from its files: the entry at `<root>` and `<root>/`, each other file at `<root>/<path>`, with its type; any other path is 404, and a window with no put is answered nothing. A POST to `/_f/v1/invoke` or `/_f/v1/action` there goes to the host as a `call`, numbered by the driver, naming the window, its `put` (the id of the `page` request whose put the calling document belongs to, from the commit of that put's entry, before its load and its answer), its op and its body's bytes in base64, only from a document of `https://cyfr.invalid` in a window whose page the driver put, the page or a frame inside it of that origin as the window's frame tree has it; a call from another window, from a frame of another origin or from a worker is failed, any other POST under the origin is 404, and a body over `call.max_body` bytes is 413, none of them passed up. The host's `answer` with the call's id is its response, with that status, the type `application/json` and that body. When the driver gives a call up before its answer, because its window navigated, showed another page or closed, it fails the request and says `callend`; an answer for a call it no longer holds is passed over. `plain_entry` is a plain page's `document`, with no head tag and a file it names relative to its `<base>`, and the `entry` the host hands for it at `href` under `nonce`: `Compendium.tincture_head_tags(Compendium.plain_entry(document), href, nonce)`, with `{sdk}` where the SDK's source (`apps/cyfr/priv/static/sdk/cyfr.js`) stands.",
